            <ArticleTitle>Caregivers of patients with chronic pain: responses to care.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>5-14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the caregiving role of nonprofessional caregivers of patients with chronic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Descriptive study of 30 caregivers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty-eight (93%) caregivers demonstrated 1 to 6 defining characteristics of caregiver role strain, and 2 (7%) had at least two factors of risk for caregiver role strain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Caring for patients with chronic pain has a meaningful impact on caregivers' lives.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Caregivers of patients with chronic pain deserve care, not only with the objective of optimizing relief for the patients, but also to help them seek and attain optimal functionality.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
